1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,098
Your CPU can wait more than 350 cycles for one number from RAM.

2
00:00:06,378 --> 00:00:09,294
That's why it keeps tiny memories inside itself.

3
00:00:10,044 --> 00:00:12,152
Take an AMD Zen four core.

4
00:00:12,432 --> 00:00:15,401
Closest of all is the level one data cache.

5
00:00:15,681 --> 00:00:19,845
It holds 32 kilobytes and answers in about 4 cycles.

6
00:00:20,595 --> 00:00:22,949
Miss there, and the core checks level two.

7
00:00:23,229 --> 00:00:26,438
That's one megabyte, about 14 cycles away.

8
00:00:27,188 --> 00:00:29,847
Next is level three, shared by the cores.

9
00:00:30,127 --> 00:00:32,115
It's roughly 50 cycles away.

10
00:00:32,865 --> 00:00:37,037
From your RAM sticks, it takes about 73 nanoseconds.

11
00:00:37,317 --> 00:00:44,334
At 5 gigahertz, that's more than 350 cycles, roughly a hundred times slower than level one.

12
00:00:45,084 --> 00:00:47,639
That's why big caches help games so much.

13
00:00:47,919 --> 00:00:51,830
The full trip is in our video, How a CPU Runs Your Code.

